  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nMidday GOES water vapor imagery showed an upper level ridge axis\nextending through the Midsouth, roughly along the I-40 corridor\nand reflected in 500mb heights around 597dam. We remained\nsituated between tropical easterly flow along the Gulf coast and\nan MCS wave train extending from KS to IL and points eastward.\n12Z LZK sounding showed 700mb temps of 13C, which is warm even\nfor mid-August.\n\nThrough Tuesday afternoon, the ridge axis will drop south near\nthe Gulf coast, while modest height falls occur over the lower OH\nRiver Valley, concurrent with an MCS moving east-southeast from\nMO. A weak surface cold front, possibly convectively-reinforced,\nwill sag into northern portions of the Midsouth toward daybreak\nMonday. A few CAMs depict isolated shower and thunderstorm\ndevelopment Monday afternoon, likely aided by the return of\nmodest deep layer shear and surface dewpoint pooling in the\nmid/upper 70s. If these dewpoints verify, 700mb temps of 10-12C\nwill likely be insufficient to cap deep moist convection\ncompletely, and some areas may experience a localized relief from\nthe heat Monday afternoon.\n\n500mb heights will continue a modest decline on Tuesday, as light\nnortherly flow prevails at the surface. Areas to the east and\nnorth of Memphis may fall short of Heat Advisory criteria Tuesday\nafternoon, which would be welcome. This relief will be\ninterrupted on Wednesday by prefrontal return flow developing\nahead of moderate midlevel height falls through the Midwest.\nThese height falls will make it to the Midsouth on Thursday,\naided by shortwave trough diving southeast from the central\nplains. By Thursday afternoon, the upper ridge will be well-\nremoved from the Midsouth and centered over the 4-corners region.\nAmong the medium range global models, there is relatively good\nconsensus in showing continued height falls and an associated\ncold frontal passage late Thursday into Friday. This would bring\na chance of thunderstorms, followed by the return of near normal \ntemperatures next weekend.",
